scope: |
  `intSum.combine(a, b)` is refused, and it should not be: the receiver IS the given instance and
  `combine` is its own member. Next target after 32ac55842 took that refusal bucket from 8 to 6.

  ALL THREE I HAVE READ ARE ONE SHAPE, checked before claiming rather than assumed from the shared
  message:

      std-monaderror:21        optionUnitError.raise[Int](())
      std-semigroup-monoid:21  intSum.combine(intSum.empty, 42)
      tagless-multi-file:39    listLogged.pure(42)

  `given intSum: Monoid[Int] with { def empty; def combine(a, b) }` — so `intSum` is an object and
  `intSum.combine` is an ordinary qualified call, which the object flattening already resolves.

  THE REFUSAL IS OVER-EAGER, not wrong in principle. `case None if instanceOnly.contains(m) &&
  !hasGiven` fires because `receiverType` cannot type the name `intSum` and `combine` is declared
  only by given instances. Both halves are true and the conclusion still does not follow: the
  receiver is not a VALUE whose type must be found, it is the instance itself.

  THE DISTINCTION THE FIX ENCODES, and it is why this is not a widening of the type search:
    extension       `xs.foldMap(f)` -> `listFoldable.foldMap(xs, f)`   receiver PREPENDED, it is
                                                                       the value operated on
    instance member `intSum.combine(a, b)` -> `intSum.combine(a, b)`   receiver NOT prepended, it
                                                                       is the owner
  `instanceDefs` already holds `object.member` for every given instance, so the test is
  `instanceDefs.contains(n + "." + m)` on an `Expr.Name` receiver — no new table, no new source of
  types, and it goes BEFORE the refusal.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 / CRASH 9, the three cases read INDIVIDUALLY against their
  expectations, and the refusal-histogram line re-read — it is the instrument that named this bucket
  and it says how many of the 6 remain.
  MEASURED 2026-08-15 AND NOT SHIPPED. The arm works and the floor says no:

      control (no arm)   PASS 218  DIFF 3  UNSUPPORTED 137  CRASH 9
      with the arm       PASS 220  DIFF 5  UNSUPPORTED 133  CRASH 9

  DIFF is a FLOOR and it rose by two. The criterion was stated BEFORE the numbers were read, so
  this is not a rule discovered to fit a result.

  THE ARM IS NOT WRONG — what it removes is a refusal that was masking other defects. The whole
  `is provided by a given instance` bucket goes from 6 to ZERO: two cases reach PASS, two move to a
  different honest refusal (an arity one), and two become DIFF. Lists compared, not counts:
  control DIFF is {parameterless-def-local, indent-block-statements, indent-config-format} and with
  the arm it is those three plus `std-index` and `std-monaderror`. Neither was PASSING before — both
  were UNSUPPORTED — so there is no PASS -> DIFF regression, but an honest refusal became a silent
  wrong answer, which is the trade this repository refuses.

  BOTH NEW DIFFS ARE ONE DEFECT, not two, and that is the finding worth keeping: `std-index` dies at
  `noneInt.handleError((_: Unit) => Some(0))` after printing nine correct lines, and `std-monaderror`
  dies on the same `handleError`. It is the extension inside `given optionUnitError:
  MonadError[Option, Unit]`, whose body is `fa match { case Some(_) => fa; case None => h(()) }`, and
  `fa` is a `val` bound to `None`. `match: no arm matched`, thrown from `Exec.prim` with no position
  — which is why it lands in DIFF rather than UNSUPPORTED.

  SO THE CLAIM SHIPS NO CODE. The arm is described in the filed entry precisely enough to re-apply
  in one edit, so the next agent does not repeat the investigation, and it lands with the
  `handleError` defect rather than before it.
